KEY TRANSLATION NOTES:
- The C function relies on the caller reusing the same buffer: the "same" check compares the new timestamp chars against the PREVIOUS call's timestamp chars. In Rust, model this with a generator struct (e.g. `UlidGen`) holding the last ULID (or last timestamp + random part), plus a public function `ulidgen_r(&mut UlidGen) -> String` or similar. Also expose a convenience that keeps a default generator (e.g. a thread-local or a simple `next_ulid()` using a static Mutex) so tests and the CLI can call it simply.
- Entropy: read 16 bytes from /dev/urandom (std::fs::File). On error, panic/abort like the C code.
- Timestamp: SystemTime::now() -> ms since epoch, encoded as 10 Crockford base32 chars (alphabet "0123456789ABCDEFGHJKMNPQRSTVWXYZ"), most significant first, exactly like the C loop (t /= 32 each step).
- Same-ms increment: replicate the C logic exactly: scan random part (16 chars) from index 15 down; while char == 'Z' set to '0'; if all were 'Z', sleep 1234567 ns (thread::sleep) and retry (loop, not unbounded recursion); otherwise advance the char to the next char in the alphabet if it is in the alphabet, else fall through to re-randomize.
- CLI: parse args manually (std only): `-n N` (default 1) and `-t`. In -t mode read lines from stdin (BufRead::lines) and print "ULID line" (note: C prints the line INCLUDING its trailing newline via getdelim; preserve that behavior — print the line as read, with its newline if present). In -n mode print N ULIDs, one per line.
- Cargo.toml: package name e.g. "ulidgen", edition 2021, lib + bin. No dependencies.

After writing the code, run `cargo build` and fix errors until it builds. Then run a quick smoke check: `cargo run -- -n 3` and `printf 'a\nb\n' | cargo run -- -t`.

Report: files created, build status, smoke test output, and any deviations from the C behavior."}

